---
layout: layouts/base.njk
title: "Yelp Rank Tracking: a hand-checked report on where you rank"
description: "Tell us the business and the search terms that bring you work. A person checks each Yelp position by hand and sends the report back, usually within a day."
permalink: /yelp-rank-tracking-tool/index.html

hero:
  eyebrow: Yelp rank tracking
  flag: Free while in beta
  title: A hand-checked report on where you rank in Yelp search.
  lede: >-
    Tell us the business and the search terms that bring you work. A person on
    our team checks each position by hand and sends the report back, usually
    within a day or so. No tool to learn, no login, no software to buy.
  cta: Request a ranking report
  note: Usually back within a day

report:
  title: Yelp position report
  place: Laguna Beach, CA
  cols: [Search term, Organic, Paid slots, Change]
  rows:
    - [emergency plumber, "3", "4", Up 4, true]
    - [water heater repair, "5", "6", Held, false]
    - [drain cleaning near me, "2", "3", Up 2, true]
    - [slab leak detection, "9", "5", Up 5, true]
  noteLabel: What we would look at first
  note: >-
    Slab leak detection sits below five competitors who all name the service on
    their page. Yours does not.
  caption: Layout is real, figures are invented.

how:
  title: How it works
  note: Checked by a person, not a crawler
  steps:
    - name: You send the business and the terms
      body: >-
        Your Yelp page and the searches that actually bring you work. Three to
        five terms is usually the right number.
    - name: A person checks each position
      body: >-
        We run each search the way a client would and record what is on the page,
        organic and sponsored. It is done by hand because that is the only way to
        see the page as it really appears.
    - name: The report usually arrives within a day
      body: >-
        One page, plain language, with a short note on what we would look at
        first. You are welcome to take it to any agency, including not us.

shows:
  title: What the report shows
  cols:
    - label: Column one
      name: Your organic position
      body: >-
        Where your page sits in the unpaid results for that term, counted from
        the top of the list. One number, checked the way a client would see it.
    - label: Column two
      name: How much of the page is paid
      body: >-
        The count of sponsored slots occupying that page. Sponsored positions
        move on nearly every search, so we count them rather than rank them, and
        the count is what informs a budget decision.
    - label: Column three
      name: Change since your last report
      body: >-
        Ask for a second report later and every term carries what it was last
        time, so movement is visible without a chart or a login.
  close: >-
    Under the table sits a short note in plain language naming the first thing we
    would change. That note is written by the person who ran the searches, not
    generated.

band:
  title: Checked by hand, one search at a time
  note: No crawler, no dashboard, no login
  image: cs-img-leaf-square.webp
  alt: A leaf, close attention

vs:
  eyebrow: Reference
  title: Do Yelp rankings vary by location?
  lede: >-
    Organic Yelp results do not vary by where the searcher is standing. A search
    for a plumber in Minneapolis returns the same organic list whether it is run
    from Minneapolis or from Tucson, because the location is a field in the
    search rather than a signal read from the device. That is the opposite of
    Google, where local results are personalised to the searcher, and it is the
    reason a single hand-checked position means something on Yelp and means very
    little on Google.
  cards:
    - name: Organic results
      kicker: Consistent, so position is worth recording
      body: >-
        The same term and the same location produce the same organic order for
        everyone. One check is a real reading, and two checks a month apart are a
        real comparison. This is why the report can be a page of numbers rather
        than a range.
    - name: Sponsored results
      kicker: Shifting, so we count them instead
      body: >-
        Paid slots rotate on nearly every search, so a sponsored position is not a
        number worth tracking. What is worth knowing is how much of the page is
        paid. Four sponsored slots above you is a different decision from one, and
        it is the honest way to weigh advertising against organic work.

limits:
  title: What this does not do
  note: Worth saying before you ask for one
  items:
    - name: It does not improve your ranking
      body: >-
        The report tells you where you stand. Moving that position is a separate
        piece of work on the profile, the categories and the reviews.
    - name: It does not monitor your reviews
      body: >-
        Positions only. New reviews, review responses and the filter are a
        different job and need watching more often than this.
    - name: It is not daily or weekly tracking yet
      body: >-
        Each report is checked by a person, so it is a snapshot you request rather
        than a feed that runs. Ask for a second one whenever you want the
        comparison.

faqTitle: Common questions
faqLink: All 50 answers in the Yelp Hub
faq:
  - q: Is the report really free?
    a: >-
      Yes, free while it is in beta. Someone here checks the positions by hand, so
      there is a real cost to us and a limit to how many we can run in a week. We
      do it because a business that sees its own numbers usually has a better
      conversation with us afterwards, whether or not it ends in work.
  - q: Do Yelp results vary depending on where the searcher is?
    a: >-
      Organic results do not. A search for a given service in a given city returns
      the same organic order whether it is run from that city or from across the
      country, because the location is typed into the search rather than read from
      the device. Sponsored slots are the exception: they rotate on nearly every
      search, which is why we count them rather than rank them.
  - q: How does Yelp rank my business?
    a: >-
      Reviews do most of the work. Yelp weighs relevance to the search and
      distance from the searched location, but among businesses that are relevant
      and close, the ones with more reviews, and with reviews arriving steadily
      rather than in a burst two years ago, sit higher. Volume, recency and rating
      all count, and only reviews that pass Yelp's filter count at all. Everything
      else is table stakes: complete categories and services, accurate hours and
      service areas, photos. Advertising buys sponsored slots, not a better
      organic position.
  - q: Does it track my reviews too?
    a: >-
      No. The report covers search positions only. Reviews need watching far more
      often than a requested snapshot allows, and they are handled as reputation
      work rather than folded in here.
  - q: Why does a Yelp ranking matter if I already rank on Google?
    a: >-
      Because they are different audiences at different moments. Yelp visitors are
      usually comparing several businesses before contacting any of them, and 74
      million people use it each month. A business can be first on Google and
      invisible on Yelp to the person who is about to choose between three names.
  - q: Can I get tracking more often than one report?
    a: >-
      Ask for another report whenever you want one and each term will carry its
      previous number. Scheduled daily or weekly tracking does not exist yet. When
      it does we will say so here rather than describe it before it is real.

form:
  flag: Free while in beta
  title: Request your ranking report
  lede: >-
    A person checks every position by hand, so the report usually comes back
    within a day or so on business days. No account, no card, no follow-up
    sequence.
  fields:
    - id: business
      label: Business name
      placeholder: As it appears on Yelp
      type: text
      autocomplete: organization
    - id: place
      label: City and state
      placeholder: Laguna Beach, CA
      type: text
      autocomplete: address-level2
    - id: name
      label: Your name
      placeholder: Who we reply to
      type: text
      autocomplete: name
    - id: email
      label: Email
      placeholder: Where the report goes
      type: email
      autocomplete: email
  termsLabel: Search terms to check
  termsHint: One per line. Three to five is usually the right number.
  cta: Send my report request
  note: Usually back within a day
---
{% from "components/container.njk" import container %}
{% from "components/button.njk" import button %}

{#
  YELP RANK TRACKING, at /yelp-rank-tracking-tool/.

  ONE OF TWO LIVE URLS, AND THE OTHER REDIRECTS HERE. The old site serves both
  this slug and /yelp-rank-tracking-landing-page, each self-canonicalising to
  itself, so the two have been splitting signals. src/_redirects 301s the other
  into this one. This slug survives because its title is a real title and its
  copy is a service page rather than a launch announcement.

  ONE INK PANE, ONE MADDER PANE. The Yelp versus Google reference is the deep
  one; the request form is the accent one, and its button is
  cs-btn--on-accent, the outlined secondary for an accent ground. A page-level
  variant there would put ink on madder at 1.99:1.

  THE FORM DOES NOT POST ANYWHERE YET, exactly like the contact page's, and
  carries the same data-form-mockup marker so both are found by one grep when a
  handler is wired. Shipping a form that silently drops a request would be worse
  than shipping none, so the marker is the honest record of that state.

  THE SAMPLE REPORT IS LABELLED AS INVENTED, in the comp's own words, because a
  table of positions that looks like a real client's is a claim.
#}

{#- 1 HERO -#}
{% call container(class="cs-rt-section cs-rt-section--first") %}
  <div class="cs-rt-hero">
    <div>
      <p class="cs-rt-hero__eyebrow">
        <span class="cs-eyebrow">{{ hero.eyebrow }}</span>
        <span class="cs-rt-flag">{{ hero.flag }}</span>
      </p>
      <h1 class="cs-rt-hero__title">{{ hero.title }}</h1>
      <p class="cs-rt-hero__lede">{{ hero.lede }}</p>
      <div class="cs-rt-hero__actions">
        {{ button(hero.cta, href="#request") }}
        <p class="cs-eyebrow">{{ hero.note }}</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    {#- The sample report. A table, because it is one. -#}
    <figure class="cs-rt-report">
      <table class="cs-rt-report__table">
        {#- The flex row is INSIDE the caption. display:flex on a caption takes
            it out of the caption slot and an anonymous cell wraps it, which
            drops it below the header row and squeezes it into one column.
            Caught by a screenshot, exactly as on the hub's rank card. -#}
        <caption class="cs-rt-report__head">
          <span class="cs-rt-report__head-row">
            <span class="cs-rt-report__title">{{ report.title }}</span>
            <span class="cs-rt-report__place">{{ report.place }}</span>
          </span>
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>{% for c in report.cols %}<th scope="col">{{ c }}</th>{% endfor %}</tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {%- for r in report.rows %}
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">{{ r[0] }}</th>
            <td>{{ r[1] }}</td>
            <td>{{ r[2] }}</td>
            <td class="cs-rt-report__delta{{ " cs-rt-report__delta--up" if r[4] }}">{{ r[3] }}</td>
          </tr>
          {%- endfor %}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="cs-rt-report__note">
        <p class="cs-eyebrow">{{ report.noteLabel }}</p>
        <p class="cs-rt-report__note-body">{{ report.note }}</p>
      </div>
      <figcaption class="cs-rt-report__caption">{{ report.caption }}</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
{% endcall %}

{#- 2 HOW IT WORKS -#}
{% call container(class="cs-rt-section") %}
  <div class="cs-rt-head">
    <h2 class="cs-rt-h2">{{ how.title }}</h2>
    <p class="cs-rt-meta">{{ how.note }}</p>
  </div>
  <ol class="cs-rt-steps">
    {%- for s in how.steps %}
    <li>
      <span class="cs-rt-steps__n" aria-hidden="true">{{ loop.index }}</span>
      <span>
        <span class="cs-rt-steps__name">{{ s.name }}</span>
        <span class="cs-rt-steps__body">{{ s.body }}</span>
      </span>
    </li>
    {%- endfor %}
  </ol>
{% endcall %}

{#- 3 WHAT THE REPORT SHOWS -#}
{% call container(class="cs-rt-section") %}
  <h2 class="cs-rt-h2 cs-rt-h2--rule">{{ shows.title }}</h2>
  <div class="cs-rt-cols">
    {%- for c in shows.cols %}
    <div class="cs-rt-cols__item">
      <p class="cs-eyebrow">{{ c.label }}</p>
      <p class="cs-rt-cols__name">{{ c.name }}</p>
      <p class="cs-rt-cols__body">{{ c.body }}</p>
    </div>
    {%- endfor %}
  </div>
  <p class="cs-rt-cols__close">{{ shows.close }}</p>
{% endcall %}

{#- image band -#}
{% call container(class="cs-rt-section") %}
  <figure class="cs-rt-band">
    <img src="{{ site.cdn }}{{ band.image }}" alt="{{ band.alt }}"
         width="1120" height="280" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
    <figcaption>
      <span class="cs-rt-band__title">{{ band.title }}</span>
      <span class="cs-eyebrow">{{ band.note }}</span>
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
{% endcall %}

{#- 4 YELP VS GOOGLE, THE ONE INK PANE -#}
<div class="cs-rt-vs">
  {% call container(class="cs-rt-vs__inner") %}
    <p class="cs-rt-vs__eyebrow">{{ vs.eyebrow }}</p>
    <h2 class="cs-rt-vs__title">{{ vs.title }}</h2>
    <p class="cs-rt-vs__lede">{{ vs.lede }}</p>
    <div class="cs-rt-vs__cards">
      {%- for c in vs.cards %}
      <div class="cs-rt-vs__card">
        <p class="cs-rt-vs__card-name">{{ c.name }}</p>
        <p class="cs-rt-vs__card-kicker">{{ c.kicker }}</p>
        <p class="cs-rt-vs__card-body">{{ c.body }}</p>
      </div>
      {%- endfor %}
    </div>
  {% endcall %}
</div>

{#- 5 WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO. The comp links two of these to spoke pages that
    do not exist, so those links are stripped rather than shipped dead. The
    third points at the form on this page and stays. -#}
{% call container(class="cs-rt-section") %}
  <div class="cs-rt-head">
    <h2 class="cs-rt-h2">{{ limits.title }}</h2>
    <p class="cs-rt-meta">{{ limits.note }}</p>
  </div>
  <div class="cs-rt-limits">
    {%- for item in limits.items %}
    <div class="cs-rt-limits__item">
      <p class="cs-rt-limits__name">{{ item.name }}</p>
      <p class="cs-rt-limits__body">{{ item.body }}</p>
      {%- if loop.last %}
      <a class="cs-rt-limits__go" href="#request">Request a report <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></a>
      {%- endif %}
    </div>
    {%- endfor %}
  </div>
{% endcall %}

{#- 6 FAQ -#}
{% call container(class="cs-rt-section") %}
  <div class="cs-rt-head">
    <h2 class="cs-rt-h2">{{ faqTitle }}</h2>
    <p class="cs-rt-meta"><a href="/yelp/">{{ faqLink }} <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></a></p>
  </div>
  <div class="cs-rt-faq">
    {%- for item in faq %}
    <details class="cs-rt-faq__item">
      <summary>{{ item.q }}</summary>
      <div class="cs-rt-faq__a"><p>{{ item.a }}</p></div>
    </details>
    {%- endfor %}
  </div>
{% endcall %}

{#- 7 FORM, THE ONE MADDER PANE -#}
{% call container(class="cs-rt-section") %}
  <div class="cs-rt-form" id="request">
    <div class="cs-rt-form__intro">
      <p class="cs-rt-flag cs-rt-flag--on-accent">{{ form.flag }}</p>
      <h2 class="cs-rt-form__title">{{ form.title }}</h2>
      <p class="cs-rt-form__lede">{{ form.lede }}</p>
    </div>
    <form class="cs-rt-form__form" data-form-mockup>
      <div class="cs-rt-form__row">
        {%- for f in form.fields %}
        <p class="cs-rt-field">
          <label class="cs-rt-field__label" for="rt-{{ f.id }}">{{ f.label }}</label>
          <input class="cs-rt-input" type="{{ f.type }}" id="rt-{{ f.id }}" name="{{ f.id }}"
                 placeholder="{{ f.placeholder }}" autocomplete="{{ f.autocomplete }}">
        </p>
        {%- endfor %}
      </div>
      <p class="cs-rt-field">
        <label class="cs-rt-field__label" for="rt-terms">{{ form.termsLabel }}</label>
        <textarea class="cs-rt-input" id="rt-terms" name="terms" rows="3"
                  aria-describedby="rt-terms-hint"></textarea>
        <span class="cs-rt-field__hint" id="rt-terms-hint">{{ form.termsHint }}</span>
      </p>
      <div class="cs-rt-form__actions">
        {{ button(form.cta, type="submit", variant="on-accent") }}
        <span class="cs-rt-form__note">{{ form.note }}</span>
      </div>
    </form>
  </div>
{% endcall %}

{#- FAQPage from the same array the visible list renders from. -#}
{%- set faqEntities = [] -%}
{%- for item in faq -%}
  {%- set _ = faqEntities.push({
    "@type": "Question",
    "name": item.q,
    "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": item.a }
  }) -%}
{%- endfor %}
<script type="application/ld+json">
{{ {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "@id": (page.url | absoluteUrl) + "#faq",
  "mainEntity": faqEntities
} | jsonld | safe }}
</script>
